mode: "100644"
text: "# The Profile Funnel\n\nThe reframe that makes this skill work: **your profile is a conversion landing page, not\ndecoration.** Content does one job — earn reach and send a curious stranger to your profile. The\n*profile* does the converting: stranger → follower → lead. Most accounts pour everything into content\nand leave a vague, leaking profile — so the visits never turn into follows or clicks.\n\nA useful diagnostic: **if a profile gets visits but few follows or link taps, it's usually the bio,\nnot the algorithm.**\n\n## The 3-second test (clarity beats cleverness)\n\nA visitor decides in seconds whether to follow. Above the fold (handle + name field + first bio line\n+ picture) the profile must instantly answer three questions:\n\n1. **WHO is this for / who do you help?**\n2. **WHAT do you do / offer?**\n3. **WHY should I follow / what do I get?**\n\nIf a stranger can't answer those at a glance, they leave. **Clarity beats cleverness every time** —\na witty-but-vague bio (\"dream big ✨ | living my best life\") converts worse than a plain, specific one\n(\"I help solo founders grow on social without burning out. Free playbook ↓\"). Be clear first;\npersonality second.\n\n## The funnel — each element serves a stage\n\nOptimize the profile as a four-stage funnel, and know which element does each job:\n\n| Stage | The job | Elements that serve it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Discovered** | be findable in search | handle, **name field / headline (searchable keywords)**, category |\n| **2. Clarity** | pass the 3-second test | name field, first bio line, profile picture |\n| **3. Follow** | earn the follow | bio (who/what/why + proof), pinned posts, highlights |\n| **4. Convert** | drive the action | link-in-bio, CTA, contact/action buttons |\n\nA profile can fail at any stage: unfindable (no keywords), unclear (vague bio), unconvincing (no\nproof/CTA), or a dead end (no/weak link). Diagnose which stage leaks and fix that first.\n\n## \"For everyone\" converts no one\n\nThe instinct to appeal to everybody is the enemy of conversion. A specific profile lets the *right*\nvisitor instantly think \"this is for me\" and follow; a generic one gives no one a reason. Niche down\n— the narrower and clearer the positioning (from `social-strategy` and `brand-profile`), the higher\nthe conversion. You're not trying to be followable by all; you're trying to be unmissable to your\npeople.\n\n## Derive it from the foundation, don't invent it\n\nThe profile is positioning made concrete. Pull from:\n- **`brand-profile` / `social-strategy`** — who you help, the wedge, the primary goal (which sets the\n  CTA and link).\n- **`audience.md`** — their language and the outcome they want (so the bio speaks to them).\n- **`voice.md`** — the tone of the bio.\n\nThe profile isn't a creative-writing exercise; it's the clearest possible statement of who you are\nand why someone should care — in the audience's words.\n\n## Honest scope\n\nThis is **advisory**: it produces the spec (handle, name, bio, link, CTA, pinned/highlights, banner\nbrief). **WoopSocial publishes posts and media — it does not edit profile fields**, so the user\napplies the spec in each app. Profile visits, link taps, and follows are not in WoopSocial post\nanalytics — judge those via **native profile insights** or the qualitative 3-second\ntest — never claimed performance data.\n"
